# `#[handlers]`

<SymbolMeta />
<SymbolSignature />

Applies to an inherent `impl` for a type declared with `#[controller]`. It consumes the method marker attributes, emits erased dispatch wrappers and route/message registrations, and leaves ordinary methods available normally.

```rust
use upwell::axum::prelude::*;

#[handlers]
impl UsersController {
    #[get("/{id}")]
    async fn get(&self, Path(id): Path<u64>) -> Json<User> { todo!() }
}
```

## Syntax

```rust
#[handlers]
#[handlers(routes_slice = ControllerRoutes)]
#[handlers(ws = Protocol)]
impl Controller { /* marked methods */ }
```

`routes_slice = Name` targets the named HTTP registry selected by `#[controller(routes_slice = Name)]`. `ws = Protocol` selects WebSocket message handling and is required when the block contains `#[message]`; it must match `#[controller(ws = Protocol)]`. The block may also use the shared `#[init]` and `#[hook(...)]` method facilities. Those lifecycle methods are not routes or messages unless they also carry the appropriate marker.

Multiple `#[handlers]` blocks may target the same controller. HTTP blocks must not create the same method/path combination. WebSocket blocks must not create the same destination for their protocol.

## Marker containment

HTTP marker attributes are valid only on methods inside an inherent `#[handlers] impl` for an HTTP controller:

- `#[get]`, `#[post]`, `#[put]`, `#[delete]`, `#[patch]`, `#[head]`, `#[options]`, and `#[route(...)]`

`#[message(...)]` is valid only in a `#[handlers(ws = Protocol)]` block for a matching WebSocket controller. The `#[topic(...)]` and `#[content]` helpers belong only inside a `#[topics]` enum and are not consumed here.

Using a route or message marker on a free function, in an unmarked impl, or on the wrong controller flavor leaves the marker with no valid consumer and produces a compile error. The markers are removed by `#[handlers]`; they are not independently executable attributes.

## Handler contract

HTTP handlers use Axum extractors. Route analysis recognizes path, query, header, JSON, form, bytes, raw form, multipart, injected values, request streams, and request context. Wire positions such as JSON/form payloads, decoded responses, and path/query values must satisfy `upwell::axum::Dto`; use `#[dto]` for application types. `Inject<T>` is server-side dependency resolution rather than wire data.

A route receiver, when present, is the shared controller receiver `&self`; controllers are singleton components. A request stream parameter is annotated `#[stream]` and a route can have at most one. Route response metadata and generated clients follow the grammar documented by `#[route]`.

Message methods may be sync or async, may be associated functions or take `&self`, have at most one non-`Inject<T>` decoded body parameter, and reject `self` and `&mut self`.
